\end{table}

\section{Cost and Sustainability Factors}
The project was developed primarily using existing personal hardware and open-source tools, which kept direct development cost low. Nevertheless, any sustained deployment would still require attention to API reliability, packaging effort, testing across platforms, maintenance time, and user support. These factors are more important at this stage than speculative revenue projection because they directly affect whether the system can be used reliably outside a controlled academic setting.

\section{Risks to Wider Adoption}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{API dependency risk:} Platform policy changes or authentication restrictions may affect integrations.
    \item \textbf{Hardware variability:} AI-assisted features may behave differently depending on the user's machine and chosen model setup.
    \item \textbf{Configuration complexity:} Advanced automation policies may require a learning curve for non-technical users.
    \item \textbf{Trust and safety concerns:} Users may hesitate to enable automation for communication tasks unless system behavior is transparent and easily reversible.
\end{itemize}

\section{Practical Growth Path}
The most realistic growth path for Auto Return is incremental. The first priority is not large-scale monetization, but technical stability, truthful documentation, and careful expansion of supported workflows. If the system is developed further, the next practical milestones would include stronger packaging, broader platform testing, clearer onboarding, and additional integrations that fit the same privacy-aware workflow philosophy.

\section{Conclusion}
From a business and deployment perspective, Auto Return should currently be viewed as a promising academic prototype with a plausible future application niche, not as a fully market-validated product. Its strongest practical advantage lies in combining communication unification, automation, and user-controlled AI workflows in a desktop environment. The main requirement for future growth is therefore not aggressive expansion planning, but continued technical refinement, validation, and usability improvement.
